### Page 4 of 7

```text
Board of County Commissioners
Minutes of the Proceedings - Final
July 15, 2025
Malm, Thompson, Heath, Hollingshead,
Zwonitzer
Nay:
Public Hearings/Rules & Reg’s
9.
PUBLIC HEARING regarding the adoption of the 2025 Laramie
County Land Use Regulations.
25 - 315
Adoption of 2025 Laramie County Land Use
Regulations
Attachments:
Chairman Malm opened the meeting for PUBLIC COMMENT.
Julie Formby said the requirement to get a permit for a home occupation was
government overreach and requested the requirement be removed.
Justin Arnold, Planning, said the current rules have a permit requirement and contain
stringent requirements on the definition of a home occupation.  He noted the permit is
free of charge and exists so people know about the provisions of a home occupation.
He said existing businesses will receive amnesty with the caveat that they will be
subject to nuisance laws.
Arnold provided further explanation on the intent of the postponement on the proposed
land use regulations.  He said they wanted to be transparent about the incorporation
of changes, one of which would provide another tool for applicants whose application
was denied by the board of commissioners to request reconsideration of the
decision. He said under current regulations, if commissioners deny a use, the
applicant has to go to district court.  He added they have been working on the
regulations for 2.5 years.
Formby asked about the impact of zone changes on property taxes and noted that
several zones would be changed to Land Use (LU).  Chairman Malm said agricultural
assessment for taxes is based on use of the land, not on zoning. Arnold added that
property owners would have to be in an unplatted parcel to claim the agricultural
exemption under statute.
Formby provided additional comment that people were not aware of the proposed
zone changes. Chairman Malm replied there have been publications, public hearings,
open houses, and social media, among other outreach methods. Arnold added that in
addition to the required public notices and open houses, more than 1700 emails were
sent, radio interviews were conducted, and he has talked with at least one in 10
constituents in unincorporated Laramie County. Commissioner Thompson stressed
the importance of feedback on the regulatory process. Formby suggested
homeowners associations could be informed.
Hearing no further PUBLIC COMMENT, Chairman Malm closed the hearing.
